      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Start by moving everything over to one side to have a quadratic equation.\n\\[x^2 - 10x + y^2 + 6y = -34\\]Moving terms that will make it easy to complete the square for each variable, we get\n\\[x^2 - 10x + 25 + y^2 + 6y + 9 + 34 = 0.\\]This expands to\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0.\\]From there, we know that $(x - 5)^2$ is positive and $(y + 3)^2$ is positive, so we know that both must be zero. This gives us $x = 5$ and $y = -3$, which allows us to find $x + y = \\boxed{-2}$.\n\nWe obtain a quadratic by moving linear terms to one side. Through completing the square in both variables, we are able to express the equation in the form of a circle; for a circle, completing the square is always rather efficient. We move terms that will make it easier to complete the square. In the example, we moved terms that would make completing the square in $x$ and $y$ easy.",
